<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vladko Maček</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vladko Maček was leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-peasant-party">Croatian Peasant Party</a> after the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a> in 1928. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cvetkovic-macek-agreement">Cvetković–Maček Agreement</a> of 1939 he secured the autonomous Banovina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and became deputy prime minister; he refused to collaborate with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> and died in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladko-macek.jpg" alt="Vladko Maček" width="320" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vladko_Ma%C4%8Dek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
